WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.790
WESLEY: Hi, and welcome to
"Serverless Migration Station."

00:00:02.790 --> 00:00:04.500
I'm Wesley from Google,
joined by Martin

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:06.210
to help you modernize
your apps running

00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:08.250
on one of our serverless
compute platforms.

00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:10.207
In this and the next
few migration modules,

00:00:10.207 --> 00:00:11.790
our little friend
Porter will take you

00:00:11.790 --> 00:00:14.460
on a journey to migrate
from App Engine taskqueues

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:15.870
to Cloud Tasks.

00:00:15.870 --> 00:00:19.080
That said, this video won't
feature any migrating at all.

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:20.400
MARTIN: Oh really, Wes?

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:21.300
Why not?

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:24.150
I thought this was the
"Serverless Migration" video

00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:24.900
series.

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:25.855
What's up with that?

00:00:25.855 --> 00:00:26.355
WESLEY: Aw.

00:00:26.355 --> 00:00:27.540
You caught me, Martin.

00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:31.530
Yeah, usually Porter does take
users from point A to point B.

00:00:31.530 --> 00:00:34.380
But here in module 7, we
need to take a detour first

00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:37.110
to reacquaint folks with
App Engine taskqueues.

00:00:37.110 --> 00:00:40.770
We'll add their queues to the
module 1 Flask Engine NEB app.

00:00:40.770 --> 00:00:44.250
Then we can migrate it to
Cloud Tasks ahead in module 8.

00:00:44.250 --> 00:00:46.060
MARTIN: OK, I get it.

00:00:46.060 --> 00:00:49.080
So we're going to take one of
our original Python 2 apps,

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:52.890
add App Engine taskqueues
to it, and then learn

00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:54.210
how to migrate to Cloud Tasks?

00:00:54.210 --> 00:00:54.900
WESLEY: Yep.

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:56.190
You got it, Martin.

00:00:56.190 --> 00:00:59.700
In module 1, we migrated from
App Engine's webapp2 framework

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:02.820
to Flask, a popular framework
in the Python community.

00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:05.160
While webapp2 apps
run on App Engine,

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:09.150
Flask apps run on App Engine
and most other hosting services.

00:01:09.150 --> 00:01:11.850
So pause here to review
the module 1 video

00:01:11.850 --> 00:01:14.730
and do its codelab to reacquaint
yourself with that migration,

00:01:14.730 --> 00:01:17.910
because this module picks up
where that one leaves off.

00:01:17.910 --> 00:01:18.990
MARTIN: Ah, yes.

00:01:18.990 --> 00:01:20.980
I remember that migration.

00:01:20.980 --> 00:01:23.220
So what are we going
to use taskqueues

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:24.868
for in our sample app, Wes?

00:01:24.868 --> 00:01:25.410
WESLEY: Yeah.

00:01:25.410 --> 00:01:26.490
Glad you asked, Martin.

00:01:26.490 --> 00:01:30.420
Well, you know our app creates a
new entity for each page visit.

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:33.450
Because it only shows the
10 most recent visits,

00:01:33.450 --> 00:01:35.820
there's no need to keep
all the old ones, right?

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:38.280
Why take up all the
extra space, and possibly

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:42.150
exceed our Datastore free
tier, and possibly have to pay?

00:01:42.150 --> 00:01:45.300
Well, we'll have to add a task
that deletes all visits older

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:47.220
than the oldest one shown.

00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:48.100
MARTIN: OK.

00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:49.800
I have to be honest.

00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:53.550
I haven't used App Engine
taskqueues in quite a while.

00:01:53.550 --> 00:01:56.040
Can I get my hands
dirty and do it myself

00:01:56.040 --> 00:01:58.770
while you're showing
us on screen?

00:01:58.770 --> 00:01:59.880
WESLEY: Absolutely.

00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:02.910
Go and grab your module 1
code if you did the codelab,

00:02:02.910 --> 00:02:05.700
or clone the repo or download
the zip file if you didn't.

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:07.500
Pause here if you
need to do that.

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:09.389
Module 1 is where we'll start.

00:02:09.389 --> 00:02:11.070
If you want to do
this by hand with me

00:02:11.070 --> 00:02:14.730
now or on your own time, please
follow the module 7 codelab.

00:02:14.730 --> 00:02:15.390
Ready?

00:02:15.390 --> 00:02:19.930
Let's go to the computer
now and do this.

00:02:19.930 --> 00:02:22.570
Since we're only adding the
use of taskqueues to our app,

00:02:22.570 --> 00:02:25.330
there are no changes to
any of the config files.

00:02:25.330 --> 00:02:28.660
Python 2 users should ensure
your lib folder is intact.

00:02:28.660 --> 00:02:30.850
If not or you're
unsure, just delete it

00:02:30.850 --> 00:02:33.340
and rerun the pip
install command.

00:02:33.340 --> 00:02:35.650
Since it's always good to
start from a working app,

00:02:35.650 --> 00:02:38.140
use gcloud app deploy to
redeploy the module 1 app

00:02:38.140 --> 00:02:41.350
and ensure it works
before we update it.

00:02:41.350 --> 00:02:43.060
This is one of the
few modules where

00:02:43.060 --> 00:02:45.280
the app will look and work
slightly differently when

00:02:45.280 --> 00:02:46.810
we're done.

00:02:46.810 --> 00:02:48.400
To delete the oldest
entries, we'll

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:51.160
use some timestamp utilities,
like those in the time

00:02:51.160 --> 00:02:52.690
and datetime modules.

00:02:52.690 --> 00:02:55.330
We're also going to be
adding logging to help debug.

00:02:55.330 --> 00:02:58.420
Most importantly, we need
to add the taskqueue API.

00:02:58.420 --> 00:03:00.550
The imports are in the
best practice ordering

00:03:00.550 --> 00:03:02.780
of standard library
packages first,

00:03:02.780 --> 00:03:04.540
followed by
third-party libraries,

00:03:04.540 --> 00:03:06.940
then finally local
libraries, and everything

00:03:06.940 --> 00:03:08.410
in alphabetical order.

00:03:08.410 --> 00:03:11.920
Since we only care about older
visits not shown to the user,

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:14.620
it has nothing to do with
when visits are created,

00:03:14.620 --> 00:03:16.750
so store_visit stays the same.

00:03:16.750 --> 00:03:19.870
The actual fetching
of most recent visits

00:03:19.870 --> 00:03:21.250
hasn't changed, either.

00:03:21.250 --> 00:03:23.860
But instead of returning
with the data right away,

00:03:23.860 --> 00:03:26.530
let's use the timestamp
of the oldest visit

00:03:26.530 --> 00:03:28.570
and delete everything
older than that.

00:03:28.570 --> 00:03:32.200
We'll save a human-readable log
entry and save it for the user.

00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:34.810
That's followed by the most
important part, creating

00:03:34.810 --> 00:03:37.390
the push task to do
the work, passing

00:03:37.390 --> 00:03:41.080
in the timestamp and the
handler the task should post to.

00:03:41.080 --> 00:03:43.510
Once the task is
added, return the data

00:03:43.510 --> 00:03:47.410
like before, but also include
the oldest visit timestamp.

00:03:47.410 --> 00:03:50.920
When the task runs, the
taskqueue service sends an HTTP

00:03:50.920 --> 00:03:55.330
POST to the handler trim(),
so let's move to that now.

00:03:55.330 --> 00:03:58.750
Flask routes
default to HTTP GET,

00:03:58.750 --> 00:04:01.540
but tasks are POSTed
to their handlers,

00:04:01.540 --> 00:04:05.320
so that's why trim()'s decorator
is designated that way.

00:04:05.320 --> 00:04:08.770
To start, it grabs the oldest
entity timestamp we got from

00:04:08.770 --> 00:04:09.850
fetch_visits().

00:04:09.850 --> 00:04:12.950
Next, it queries for all
entities older than that.

00:04:12.950 --> 00:04:16.360
We don't need the data,
so just ask for keys only

00:04:16.360 --> 00:04:17.350
to speed it up.

00:04:17.350 --> 00:04:21.620
If old visits exist, log how
many we're deleting and do it.

00:04:21.620 --> 00:04:23.530
Otherwise, log there aren't any.

00:04:23.530 --> 00:04:25.450
And that's it for the task.

00:04:25.450 --> 00:04:28.630
The main handler stores and
fetches visits as before,

00:04:28.630 --> 00:04:31.810
but now we have a timestamp
for the oldest displayed visit

00:04:31.810 --> 00:04:33.940
to let the user know
that visits older

00:04:33.940 --> 00:04:36.550
than that will
automatically be deleted.

00:04:36.550 --> 00:04:38.830
Both the data and
oldest visit timestamp

00:04:38.830 --> 00:04:40.630
are sent to the
template for rendering.

00:04:40.630 --> 00:04:44.300
But before we do that, we
better update that template.

00:04:44.300 --> 00:04:46.340
The majority of the
template stays the same,

00:04:46.340 --> 00:04:48.090
but check for a timestamp.

00:04:48.090 --> 00:04:50.780
If so, show that older
visits will be deleted.

00:04:50.780 --> 00:04:53.510
Because it checks for the
existence of the timestamp,

00:04:53.510 --> 00:04:56.030
this template can be
used in other modules,

00:04:56.030 --> 00:04:57.770
because there are
no side effects

00:04:57.770 --> 00:04:59.520
if the timestamp isn't there.

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:01.820
And those are all the
changes necessary to add

00:05:01.820 --> 00:05:03.920
push tasks to our existing app.

00:05:03.920 --> 00:05:06.620
If you did everything correctly
and redeployed the sample app,

00:05:06.620 --> 00:05:08.840
you should now see an
additional message down

00:05:08.840 --> 00:05:10.820
at the bottom letting
users know what visits

00:05:10.820 --> 00:05:12.650
were deleted by the push task.

00:05:12.650 --> 00:05:15.190
OK, back to the program.

00:05:15.190 --> 00:05:16.860
MARTIN: Thanks for
the tutorial, Wes.

00:05:16.860 --> 00:05:19.140
Where should I go if I
want to reacquaint myself

00:05:19.140 --> 00:05:20.770
with App Engine taskqueues?

00:05:20.770 --> 00:05:22.650
WESLEY: Well,
Martin, I'd say start

00:05:22.650 --> 00:05:24.990
with the taskqueue
overview page in the docs.

00:05:24.990 --> 00:05:27.780
While our simple app
only uses push tasks,

00:05:27.780 --> 00:05:30.690
you'll find docs for both
push and pull queues there.

00:05:30.690 --> 00:05:32.950
MARTIN: Cool, I'll take a look.

00:05:32.950 --> 00:05:36.480
But can you give me a heads-up
on what we're doing next?

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:37.380
WESLEY: Sure, Martin.

00:05:37.380 --> 00:05:40.200
Now that we have App Engine
taskqueues in our app,

00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:43.380
we'll migrate it to Cloud
Tasks ahead in module 8.

00:05:43.380 --> 00:05:45.750
If you want to read
ahead, check out the links

00:05:45.750 --> 00:05:47.970
to the migration page, as
well as the Cloud Tasks

00:05:47.970 --> 00:05:49.140
documentation.

00:05:49.140 --> 00:05:51.117
You can also do the
module 8 codelab

00:05:51.117 --> 00:05:52.950
if you want to get
started on it right away,

00:05:52.950 --> 00:05:55.350
or go back to the repo to
either look at the code

00:05:55.350 --> 00:05:57.060
or check out the next video.

00:05:57.060 --> 00:06:00.000
And we look forward to having
all of you join us for module 8

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:01.680
and moving to Cloud Tasks.

00:06:01.680 --> 00:06:02.430
This is Wesley.

00:06:02.430 --> 00:06:04.110
On behalf of Martin
and Porter, we

00:06:04.110 --> 00:06:06.420
hope to see you at that
next "Migration Station"

00:06:06.420 --> 00:06:09.240
or on another "Serverless
Expedition" soon.

00:06:09.240 --> 00:06:12.590
[MUSIC PLAYING]

